  "query_preview": "..\n\nTonight through Friday...\n\nWeak west/southwest flow will bring some disturbances into our\nregion today. As highs peak in the upper 70s late today, we \nshould see convective initiation come off the higher terrain into\nthe foothills and nearby lower elevations as they track slowly \neast in the evening. A few storms may be capable of brief heavy \nrain with the projected MUCAPE 500-1000 J/kg and PWATs around 0.85\ninches. Convection should weaken after dark and drift east \novernight as isolated light showers or sprinkles. Dewpoint\ndepressions suggest patchy fog is again possible later tonight \ninto early Sunday morning, particularly in the eastern river \nvalleys and out west in the Paradise Valley/Livingston vicinity.\n\nCAMs indicate the possibility of a few strong storms in\nour far east (Fallon, Carter Counties) Sunday as some energy \nexits the state where MUCAPE values push 1000 J/kg and PWATs of \nover 1.0 inches exist. In the meantime, some weaker isolated to \nscattered activity is expected over the mountains and western \nportions of the CWA in the afternoon. Highs Sunday will warm into \nthe lower to mid 80s. \n\nFor the work week ahead, look for seasonal temps mainly in the\n80s as high pressure aloft prevails. A weak cold front Tuesday\nwill drop temps a bit to upper 70s and lower 80s, and produce the\nbest chance of precipitation for the week (25-50% coverage), but \notherwise look for just a slight chance of showers each day, \nmainly over the high country. Temps will trend gradually up\npushing 90 by Friday. BT",
